/*  The snake game — the vertical slice: model, view and clock each in their own file, and a main
    that wires them.

    ⚠ Not a game yet. Nothing reads a key and nothing decides anything; the body moves because
    the frame number says so. What it proves is the structure — a type crossing two file
    boundaries, a view that takes model values as parameters, a clock the game owns, and a
    blueprint that builds all four together.

    ★ The view is its own file as of 2026-09-20. It could not be before: a declaration inside a
    pull was unable to name a pulled type in its SIGNATURE, so `frame-of, given (the series of
    spot body)` was refused and the drawing had to live here. Splitting it out is what the fix
    bought, and it is the difference between files and layers.
*/

Pull books on screen, and board, and pacing.
    For each frame in range 0 to 1, repeat:
        Define body as a series of spot.
        Insert cast board's at on (4, 8 + frame) into body.
        Insert cast board's at on (4, 9 + frame) into body.
        Insert cast board's at on (4, 10 + frame) into body.

        State "frame {frame + 1}:".
        For each line in cast screen's frame-of on (body), repeat:
            State line.
        Done.

        /* The clock earning its place: without this the two frames arrive together. */
        Cast pacing's nap on (40).
    Done.
Done.
